'''Plastic Beach''' ({{lang|ca|Platja de Plàstic}}), officially the '''Metallúrgia Coastal Research Cluster''' ({{lang|ca|Clúster d'Investigació Costaner de Metallúrgia}}), is an industrial research and technology campus in the [[Metallúrgia]] neighbourhood of [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]], [[Sant Cristòfor]]. The cluster occupies the site of the former Dàrsena metalworks, a steel and heavy engineering complex that operated from 1910 until its closure in 1934. Since the 2000s, the area has been redeveloped as a campus for companies in plastics, biotechnology, and chemical research, housed primarily in converted warehouse buildings, with several new office towers under construction as of 2026.

The name "Plastic Beach" emerged informally among residents and workers in reference to the concentration of plastics and chemical companies in the coastal area, and has been adopted in common usage despite not being the official designation. The Municipal Council of Dàrsena officially designated the area as the Metallúrgia Coastal Research Cluster in 2020.

== History ==

=== Dàrsena metalworks (1910–1934) ===

The Metallúrgia neighbourhood takes its name from the Dàrsena metalworks ({{lang|ca|Metal·lúrgia de Dàrsena}}), a steel and heavy engineering complex established in 1910 on the northern coast of Dàrsena, on the southern shore of the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]]. The metalworks operated blast furnaces, foundries, and structural steel fabrication facilities, supplying the construction sector of the growing city and the Zong colonial administration's infrastructure projects. At its peak in the 1920s, the complex employed approximately 3,000 workers and was one of the largest industrial sites in the Sant Cristòfor area.

The metalworks declined during the Great Adonian War, when the siege of Sant Cristòfor disrupted operations and damaged facilities. The complex never fully recovered after the war and was permanently closed in 1934. The site remained largely abandoned for decades, with the warehouse buildings and foundry shells standing as industrial ruins along the coast. The surrounding neighbourhood, which had been built to house metalworkers, declined into a low-income residential area.

=== Warehouses (1934–1980s) ===

The closure of the metalworks left a large tract of industrial land on the Dàrsena coast. The site remained largely unused through the 1930s and 1940s, with the warehouse buildings and foundry shells standing as industrial ruins along the coast. The surrounding neighbourhood, which had been built to house metalworkers, declined into a low-income residential area.

From the 1950s through the 1980s, the site was used as warehouse space, with the former foundry buildings and storage sheds repurposed for freight handling, storage, and small-scale industrial activity serving the nearby harbour. The surrounding neighbourhood remained working-class and largely overlooked during the post-war period.

=== Loft conversions (1980s–2000s) ===

From the 1980s onward, as Dàrsena was progressively deindustrialised — the steel mill closed in 1982, the chemical plant in 1987 — the warehouse buildings of the Metallúrgia area began to be converted into loft apartments and small offices, part of the broader urban regeneration of Dàrsena. However, the coastal stretch of Metallúrgia developed more slowly than the rest of Dàrsena, owing to the presence of contaminated industrial land and limited waterfront access. While the rest of Dàrsena became the city's nightlife and residential centre, Metallúrgia remained a quieter mix of converted lofts, small offices, and remaining warehouse space.

=== Research cluster (2000s–present) ===

The transformation of the Metallúrgia area began in the 2000s, when plastics and chemical companies began moving into the converted warehouse and loft spaces, attracted by low rents, large floor plates, and proximity to the harbour and canal corridor. The area's industrial infrastructure — heavy power supply, reinforced floors, and loading docks — proved well-suited to laboratory and pilot-scale manufacturing use. [[Polímers de Balboa]] (PDB) relocated its Sant Cristòfor research division to a converted warehouse in Metallúrgia in the 2000s, citing the need for larger laboratory space and proximity to the university research community. [[Elastòmers de Balboa]] (EDB) established its Sant Cristòfor R&D laboratory in the cluster, having previously operated from a smaller facility in the Dàrsena industrial district.

In 2012, an infill [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro]] station was added at Metallúrgia on Lines 5 and 6, improving transit access to the area and supporting further growth. The [[University of Sant Cristòfor]] established a biochemical research building in the neighbourhood in 2014 for graduate students in biochemistry, chemical engineering, and materials science, providing an academic anchor that attracted further private investment.

In 2018, the Municipal Council of Dàrsena formally designated the Metallúrgia area as a research and technology zone, offering reduced property taxes and expedited permitting for companies in plastics, biotechnology, and chemical research, consolidating the cluster's organic growth under a formal framework. The Municipal Council of Dàrsena officially designated the area as the Metallúrgia Coastal Research Cluster in 2020. Several biotechnology companies and chemical research firms moved into the area in the late 2010s and early 2020s, attracted by the cluster effect of co-location with related companies and the university presence.

As of 2026, the cluster comprises approximately 25 companies and research groups, employing approximately 3,200 people. The majority are housed in converted warehouse buildings, while several new office towers are under construction along the coastal edge of the site, scheduled for completion between 2027 and 2029.

== Geography ==

Plastic Beach occupies approximately 0.8 square kilometres on the northern coast of [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]], along the southern shore of the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]]. The site is bounded by the coastal road to the south, the former Dàrsena rail freight yard to the east, and the Badia de Sant Cristòfor to the north. The terrain is flat, reflecting its industrial origins as a reclaimed coastal site.

The neighbourhood is served by the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro]], with Lines 5 and 6 crossing Dàrsena. An infill station was added at Metallúrgia in 2012, providing direct rapid transit access to the cluster.

== Companies ==

The cluster's tenant companies and institutions fall into three main sectors, alongside the academic anchor of the University of Sant Cristòfor:

=== Academic ===

* [[University of Sant Cristòfor]]: The university's biochemical research building, established in 2014 for graduate students in biochemistry, chemical engineering, and materials science. The building provides laboratory space, graduate student offices, and shared analytical facilities, and serves as the academic anchor of the cluster. The university maintains research partnership programmes with cluster companies, providing access to university facilities and graduate student placements.

=== Plastics and polymers ===

* [[Polímers de Balboa]] (PDB): The cluster's largest tenant. PDB's Sant Cristòfor research division, relocated from the Dàrsena manufacturing site in 2019, occupies a converted warehouse complex of approximately 12,000 square metres. The division conducts research on advanced polymer formulations, recycling technologies, and engineering plastics for the automotive and electronics sectors. Approximately 180 researchers and support staff.
* [[Elastòmers de Balboa]] (EDB): R&D laboratory established in 2020, developing elastomer formulations, conducting product testing and certification, and working on process improvement. Approximately 100 employees.
* [[Corporació de plàstics Jala]] (CPJ): Pilot-scale research facility for biodegradable plastics, established 2022. Approximately 40 employees.

=== Biotechnology ===

* [[Nutrició del Món]]: The agricultural nutrition and fertiliser company maintains a research office in the cluster, focused on biochemical applications in crop nutrition. Approximately 30 employees.
* [[BioDàrsena]]: A biotechnology startup focused on industrial enzymes for plastic recycling and biodegradation. Founded in 2021 by researchers from the University of Sant Cristòfor. Approximately 25 employees.
* [[Genètica de l'Istme]]: Agricultural biotechnology company developing crop varieties for the Balboan climate. Established in the cluster in 2023. Approximately 35 employees.
* [[Laboratoris Costa de Dàrsena]]: Contract research organisation providing analytical and testing services to the pharmaceutical and chemical industries. Approximately 60 employees.

=== Chemical research ===

* [[Companyia de Productes Químics Pesants Balboa]] (PQQB): Applied research laboratory for specialty chemicals, established 2023. Approximately 50 employees.
* [[Companyia Química Occidental]] (CQO): Pilot plant and research facility for industrial solvents and surfactants. Approximately 30 employees.
* [[Grup Químic Central]] (GQC): Formulation laboratory for specialty chemical compounds. Approximately 20 employees.

== Development ==

The cluster's development has been driven by a combination of private investment and public incentives:

* '''Municipal tax incentives''': The Dàrsena Municipal Council designated the Metallúrgia area as a research and technology zone in 2018, offering reduced property taxes for qualifying companies for an initial period of ten years.
* '''Provincial redevelopment grants''': The Estret provincial government provided grants for the remediation of contaminated industrial land and the conversion of warehouse buildings for research use.
* [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB): The sovereign wealth fund has invested in the cluster's infrastructure, including the construction of a shared laboratory facility and the installation of high-capacity power and data infrastructure.
* '''University presence''': The University of Sant Cristòfor's biochemical research building, established in 2014, provided an academic anchor that attracted private companies to the area and continues to supply graduate talent and research collaboration.

=== New construction ===

As of 2026, three new office towers are under construction along the coastal edge of the cluster:

* '''Metallúrgia Tower''' (22 storeys, completion 2027): A mixed-use office and laboratory building, the first new construction in the cluster. The tower will provide approximately 18,000 square metres of office and laboratory space.
* '''Platja Tower''' (18 storeys, completion 2028): An office tower targeting biotechnology and pharmaceutical companies, with specialised laboratory flooring and ventilation systems.
* '''Dàrsena Coast Tower''' (28 storeys, completion 2029): The tallest planned building in the cluster, providing office space for larger companies and shared conference and meeting facilities. The tower is being developed by a consortium of cluster tenants and the FIDB.

The new towers represent a shift from the cluster's warehouse-based origins toward a more conventional office park model, which has generated some debate among existing tenants about the character of the area. Some early tenants have expressed concern that rising rents associated with the new construction may displace smaller companies and startups, echoing the gentrification dynamics that affected the rest of Dàrsena in the 1990s.

